title: read one field across every ticket, in one pass
effort: S
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T06:21:54Z
updated: 2026-09-17T06:21:54Z

Nothing in the CLI reads a field across tickets. list reads the first three
lines, tree reads the title, and a question like "what is the effort spread
of the open work?" or "which tickets have no area?" is a shell loop today.

  pidly field <name> [-a]     number and value, one line each

One awk pass over every info file, matching the field by name, skipping
closed work unless -a. A ticket without the field prints - so the gap is
visible: that is usually the question being asked.

Aggregation is then sort | uniq -c on the output, which is why this prints
rows rather than summarising. A --count that tallies is a later decision, if
the one-liner turns out to be the thing everyone writes.

The pass is the point. Measured over 2000 tickets: one awk reading a fixed
line number is 0.43s, the same pass matching the field by name is 0.44s, and
a process per ticket is 17.4s. So the win is one pass rather than one spawn,
and it does not need fixed positions — the field block is under ten lines
and ends at a blank. Match by name; a scanning reader is correct even for a
ticket that predates the current schema, and a positional one is silently
wrong for exactly those.

Avoid nextfile: it is not POSIX. Track per-file completion in the awk
program instead, which measured the same.

Done when the command exists, reads only the field block, prints - for a
missing field, and the tests cover an open-only default, -a, a field no
ticket has, and a tracker whose tickets disagree about which fields they
carry.
